<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indravarma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1st century BCE–1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Indravarman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Indravarma was a ruler of the Apracharaja dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bajaur">Bajaur</a> in the north-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> around the turn of the era. He is known from coins and from a Buddhist reliquary he dedicated, the Bajaur casket. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aspavarma">Aspavarma</a> later served as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> and Indo-Parthian overlords.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indravarma.jpg" alt="Indravarma" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apracharajas_Aspavarama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
